  "summary": "Recent preprints link endothelial phosphorylation switches, perivascular stromal support and conserved fibrosis matrix programs to tissue integrity.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how endothelial and stromal cells regulate tissue architecture through signalling-dependent RNA-level and matrix programs. In endothelial cells, VEGF-A triggers phosphorylation of Phf6 at serine 199, which acts as a molecular switch reshaping RNA binding to promote angiogenesis. In zebrafish, the endothelial adaptor She recruits collagen-producing perivascular stromal cells that maintain dorsal aorta stability. In human atrial fibrillation, single-nucleus multi-omics identifies fibroblast and cardiomyocyte states, with JMJD1C implicated as a mediator of fibroblast remodelling. A cross-organ analysis of human and mouse fibrosis RNA-seq data defines a conserved 25-gene matrix-remodelling core, although gene-level cross-species concordance is weak. What is new across these preprints is the resolution of post-translational and RNA-binding control in endothelial signalling, and the attribution of matrix-producing stromal support to vascular stability.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
